WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.680
Which Transformer was only added as&nbsp;
a marketing gimmick for Chevy? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.460
was the hardest scene Michael Bay shot&nbsp;
for the entire series? Roll into these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.704
awesome details from the billion-dollar franchise.

00:00:10.704 --> 00:00:14.460
The "Transformers" movies went on to&nbsp;
become massive hits at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.400
but they weren't originally an area of intense&nbsp;
focus for producer Don Murphy. Back in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.480
Murphy explained to Latino Review that his&nbsp;
original goal when it came to turning toys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.340
into massive live-action movies was the&nbsp;
"G.I. Joe" franchise. Murphy had undergone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.480
lengthy conversations with Hasbro about&nbsp;
acquiring the film rights to the property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.700
and Sony Pictures was even floating the idea&nbsp;
of distributing this potential film adaptation.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.160
However, real-world events soon cut Murphy's&nbsp;
ambitions for "G.I. Joe" short. The invasion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.960
Iraq suddenly made the prospect of adapting that&nbsp;
specific toy into a globally appealing franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.180
much less feasible. The then-head of Hasbro&nbsp;
Films, Carol Monroe, proceeded to point Murphy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.220
in the direction of the "Transformers" property&nbsp;
as another Hasbro IP he could adapt into a film.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.480
While Murphy didn't have any childhood&nbsp;
nostalgia for "Transformers," he did know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.860
that other close creative partners, like&nbsp;
producer Tom DeSanto, harbored a passion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.720
for the franchise. Recognizing the fanbase&nbsp;
for those robots in disguise, Murphy got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.160
work on getting "Transformers" off the ground.&nbsp;
The result was a pop culture phenomenon that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.401
would've never happened if Murphy's initial&nbsp;
"G.I. Joe" plans had gotten off the ground.

00:01:21.401 --> 00:01:22.560
"Now I know."

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.498
"And knowing is half the battle."

00:01:24.498 --> 00:01:28.860
Michael Bay's stabs at "Transformers" movies&nbsp;
vary wildly in the characters they chronicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:34.200
and even the time periods they travel to, but they&nbsp;
all tend to share one quality: a focus on human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.600
characters. We begin these tales with human&nbsp;
beings like Sam Witwicky and Cade Yeager and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.100
view gigantic sci-fi adventures largely through&nbsp;
their eyes. With each new "Transformers" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.420
the robotic characters get to be more and&nbsp;
more prominent, but the humans are still at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.140
the forefront of the narrative. Believe it&nbsp;
or not, there was a point in time when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.520
original live-action "Transformers" movie would've&nbsp;
been even more reliant on its human characters.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.320
Screenwriter Roberto Orci explained&nbsp;
to IGN that the very first draft of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.520
"Transformers" was focused almost solely on&nbsp;
the lead human characters portrayed by Shia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.080
LaBeouf and Megan Fox. This version of the&nbsp;
story leaned more heavily on the realistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.700
ramifications of a kid buying a car that&nbsp;
turns out to be a robotic alien in disguise.

00:02:14.886 --> 00:02:18.786
"That car is sensitive. I mean, four&nbsp;
thousand dollars just drove off."

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.520
Orci and company quickly realized that&nbsp;
the inaugural draft just wasn't clicking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.720
and subsequent rewrites took the skeleton&nbsp;
of Sam Witwicky's storyline from the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.040
pass and injected more material separate&nbsp;
from this character. It's understandable:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.105
Of course "Transformers" fans would want&nbsp;
more robots in "Transformers" movies.

00:02:36.105 --> 00:02:41.280
While iconic "Transformers" robots like Optimus&nbsp;
Prime and Bumblebee made it to the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.920
live-action storytelling with "Transformers" in&nbsp;
2007, other fixtures of the franchise were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:52.380
so lucky. One example was Arcee. One of the most&nbsp;
famous lady robots in the entire "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.660
canon, Arcee would've seemed like a slam dunk for&nbsp;
inclusion into a big "Transformers" blockbuster.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.640
"And you better stay close to me."

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:06.401
"No, you'd better stay close to me."

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.740
However, she was nowhere to be found&nbsp;
in the franchise's first installment.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:16.020
Screenwriter Roberto Orci revealed via IGN&nbsp;
that Arcee was originally planned to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.700
part of "Transformers," but female Transformers&nbsp;
were a concept that required too much time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.140
sufficiently explain in that original movie.&nbsp;
Wanting to avoid any potential complications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:31.020
Orci decided to leave Arcee on the cutting room&nbsp;
floor and save her for another day. Arcee would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.340
eventually get to join the live-action incarnation&nbsp;
of "Transformers" through subsequent movies like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.161
"Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen"&nbsp;
and "Transformers: Rise of the Beasts."

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.800
Today, some directors tasked with helming&nbsp;
a blockbuster movie adaptation of a beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.180
nerd property are bound to spend a lot of time&nbsp;
talking to the press about how much they love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.440
it. Michael Bay, though, has never been one&nbsp;
to play by conventional filmmaking rules.

00:03:52.543 --> 00:03:57.346
"The movie can not have tracks.&nbsp;
See the tracks? See the tracks?"
 &nbsp;

00:03:57.346 --> 00:04:03.000
It's no surprise, then, that Bay has always been&nbsp;
open about how he was downright contemptuous of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.980
the "Transformers" characters before he signed&nbsp;
on to direct "Transformers" in the mid-2000s.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.700
As Collider reported, Bay recalled that&nbsp;
he missed the boat on falling in love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.080
with "Transformers" back in the 1980s because&nbsp;
he was too young. When he was first offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.800
the chance to direct "Transformers" and&nbsp;
attend a seminar at Hasbro outlining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:24.540
franchise's lengthy history, Bay remained&nbsp;
skeptical of the property. As he recalled:

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.960
"I really thought, ‘What the f--- am&nbsp;
I going to Hasbro for Transformers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.720
school?' I thought I was going&nbsp;
to learn how to fold up robots."

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.800
However, once he dove deeper into&nbsp;
"Transformers" lore, Bay was entranced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.960
with the universe. From there, Bay became a&nbsp;
massive "Transformers" devotee, though he still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.206
sought to make the movie appealing to the kind&nbsp;
of "Transformers" non-fans that he used to be.

00:04:44.206 --> 00:04:48.540
"Transformers" obviously wasn't Michael&nbsp;
Bay's first stab at big-budget filmmaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.300
Thanks to productions like "Armageddon" and "Pearl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.860
Harbor," Bay had already become synonymous&nbsp;
with expensive spectacle movies. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.840
"Transformers" was no mere retread of what he'd&nbsp;
done in the past. Most notably, Bay's blockbuster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.380
filmmaking efforts before "Transformers"&nbsp;
all centered on flesh-and-blood people.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.060
That changed with "Transformers," which hinged&nbsp;
its plot on towering robots that were brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.250
into the live-action world by CGI wizardry. Bay&nbsp;
worked with animators closely and constantly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:19.260
post-production to make sure characters were as&nbsp;
vividly alive as possible. Having to navigate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:24.420
finer nuances of animated filmmaking was uncharted&nbsp;
territory for Bay at this juncture in his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:29.134
but it was that unfamiliarity that excited&nbsp;
him when he set out to helm "Transformers."

00:05:29.134 --> 00:05:33.480
Forget the Decepticons; one of the biggest&nbsp;
foes the Transformers had to overcome was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:39.360
the Writers Guild of America strike from the&nbsp;
end of 2007 to the very start of 2008. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.720
event disrupted the entire film and&nbsp;
television industry in North America;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.120
few projects were safe. Not even mega-budgeted&nbsp;
tentpoles like "Transformers: Revenge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.260
Fallen" could be counted on to escape the&nbsp;
writers' strike unscathed. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.520
director Michael Bay has been quite vocal about&nbsp;
how many "Revenge of the Fallen" shortcomings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.960
emerged because of the attempts to make this&nbsp;
blockbuster in the middle of the writers' strike.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.160
Bay explained to Empire that the pressure&nbsp;
of an impending writers' strike ensured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.580
that the screenplay for "Revenge of the&nbsp;
Fallen" was put together too hastily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.740
with the primary narrative assembled in just three&nbsp;
weeks. Not only did this provide a time crunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:18.300
for creating a story in the first place, but the&nbsp;
lengthy nature of the strike ensured that "Revenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.200
of the Fallen" missed out on several months&nbsp;
where its screenplay could've been polished.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.981
"I am directly below the enemy's scrotum."

00:06:27.981 --> 00:06:31.860
Bay didn't lay every flaw in "Revenge of the&nbsp;
Fallen" at the feet of the writers' strike.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.360
He also blamed the film's incorporation of&nbsp;
more fantastical elements as a key reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:41.449
it came up short. However, it's clear the&nbsp;
writers' strike did this movie no favors.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:46.140
It's often hard to tell the various robots apart&nbsp;
from one another in the Michael Bay "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.460
movies thanks to the character designs, as&nbsp;
well as the rapid-fire editing style of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:55.380
features. But even die-hard fans of this franchise&nbsp;
would be hard-pressed to name or remember Jolt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.720
a blue Autobot who transforms into a Chevy Volt.&nbsp;
Jolt's screen time in "Transformers: Revenge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.960
the Fallen" is minimal, he has no dialogue, and&nbsp;
his only personality trait is having electricity.

00:07:06.101 --> 00:07:08.961
"Jolt! Electrify."

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.280
Turns out, the character's lack of noticeable&nbsp;
traits was a byproduct of how late Jolt was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.140
added into the script. Screenwriter Roberto Orci&nbsp;
explained to The Hollywood Reporter that Chevy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.280
insisted that there be a robot in "Revenge of&nbsp;
the Fallen" that could help promote the Chevy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.027
Volt. Considering these circumstances,&nbsp;
it's no wonder Jolt was so forgettable.

00:07:28.356 --> 00:07:32.160
"Transformers: Age of Extinction" didn't&nbsp;
address or correct any long-standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:36.420
problems fans and general audiences alike had&nbsp;
expressed with the "Transformers" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.260
but that didn't seem to hurt the&nbsp;
movie's box office prospects. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:44.460
"Age of Extinction" became the biggest&nbsp;
feature of 2014 at the worldwide box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:50.100
and the only title from that year to crack $1&nbsp;
billion globally. Further sequels were inevitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:54.720
but given how lucrative this franchise was,&nbsp;
Paramount Pictures wasn't just settling for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.160
doing a straightforward sequel. This studio&nbsp;
was now harboring ambitions of "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:04.320
becoming the next Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;
an expansive saga spawning countless spinoffs.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.660
In March 2015, news broke that Akiva Goldsman&nbsp;
and Michael Bay, among others, were spearheading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:13.740
a writer's room that would figure out multiple&nbsp;
different projects set within the "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.700
universe. The ambitions were high, and the&nbsp;
dense lore attached to the "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:22.440
franchise beyond the live-action movies gave&nbsp;
these potential new features plenty of material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:27.180
to utilize. However, the potential of this&nbsp;
writer's room has yet to fully come to fruition.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.880
While the spinoff movie "Bumblebee" did premiere&nbsp;
in 2018, it took another five years for "Rise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:36.600
the Beasts" to jump into the fray. The&nbsp;
underwhelming box office performance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.460
"Transformers: The Last Knight" in 2017 probably&nbsp;
forced Paramount to pump the brakes on those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:45.630
big plans for an endless "Transformers"&nbsp;
cinematic universe, at least for a while.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.894
"What was that?"
 
"That was a mistake."

00:08:48.894 --> 00:08:54.360
Michael Bay is no stranger to battle sequences,&nbsp;
especially after doing a decade of "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.800
movies. But the filmmaker explained to&nbsp;
Fandango that the fifth entry in this saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.060
"Transformers: The Last Knight," contained&nbsp;
the most arduous action sequence in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.680
career. Bay openly admitted that he'd never&nbsp;
done a period-era medieval battle involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.700
knights before he shot the opening fight&nbsp;
sequence of "Transformers: The Last Knight."

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.620
Most of Bay's big action sequences involved car&nbsp;
chases and elements discernibly attached to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:21.240
modern world. Suddenly being thrust into the&nbsp;
landscape and battle tactics of a period-era&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.400
setting was an incredibly daunting task for&nbsp;
Bay. Bay also had personal problems informing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:30.900
why this sequence was so challenging, as his&nbsp;
dog Bonecrusher, who had appeared in several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:35.400
previous Transformers films, died the night&nbsp;
before he shot this sequence. The loss of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.260
pet impacted Bay profoundly, and it made the&nbsp;
concept of going to shoot a big action sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:45.120
the very next day a surreal experience.&nbsp;
However, with so many practical tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:49.560
including countless horses and extras, ready&nbsp;
to go for the shoot, Bay had to buckle down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:54.600
and direct a battle sequence like no other in his&nbsp;
filmography. The result was a set piece that took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:59.346
three days to shoot but did leave Bay feeling&nbsp;
incredibly accomplished once it was finished.

00:09:59.346 --> 00:10:05.040
Michael Bay spent an entire decade of his life&nbsp;
making "Transformers" movies. A decade is a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.020
chunk of time, and while Bay didn't exclusively&nbsp;
direct "Transformers" movies over those 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.700
he still dedicated most of his life in that&nbsp;
timespan to battling robots. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.820
with the benefit of hindsight, Bay&nbsp;
has a lot of complicated thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.460
about immersing himself in one&nbsp;
fictional world for so long.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.840
Bay reflected to Unilad that he did have&nbsp;
some regrets about being so ingrained in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.060
the "Transformers" world for so long. He said:

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:31.680
"I made too many of them. Steven Spielberg&nbsp;
said, 'Just stop at three'. And I said I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.580
stop. The studio begged me to do a&nbsp;
fourth, and then that made a billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:40.200
too. And then I said I'm gonna stop here. And&nbsp;
they begged me again. I should have stopped."

00:10:40.201 --> 00:10:45.613
"Five's good. Five's good. It is sad, you're&nbsp;
right. It is sad, okay? But five's good."

00:10:45.613 --> 00:10:49.680
Before he directed "Bumblebee," Travis&nbsp;
Knight was a director and artist strictly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.600
associated with stop-motion animated features.&nbsp;
Specifically, he was the man running Laika,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.920
the outfit behind groundbreaking films like&nbsp;
"Coraline" and "Kubo and the Two Strings,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.120
the latter of which Knight also directed.&nbsp;
His career up to this point was built on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.500
totally original animated properties, which&nbsp;
made the idea of him helming a live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.640
spinoff of the "Transformers" movies a&nbsp;
puzzling proposition. However, Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:15.780
explained to Polygon that he'd been a massive&nbsp;
"Transformers" fan ever since he was a kid.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:20.700
This especially informed the prologue set on&nbsp;
Cybertron, which was jam-packed with shoutouts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:26.220
to vintage "Transformers" media, such as a more&nbsp;
classical depiction of Soundwave. On paper, Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:30.480
may have seemed like a strange choice to helm&nbsp;
a "Transformers" spinoff. But his adoration for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.627
those robots in disguise ensured that he was just&nbsp;
right to give "Bumblebee" a spin as its director.

